WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
- If you're talking to Elon or
you wanna have a conversation

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.240
with Elon and you get that
opportunity to talk to Elon,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.830
you need to be concise, quick.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.430
- There's definitely 2:00 AM texts

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.830
and 6:00 AM conference calls.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.090
- When I used to go
into meetings with him,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.980
I used to always stop by our barista

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.070
and I would get a couple
espressos and chug that down

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:17.940
and then go into the meeting.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.829
- He has never said this,
but I've watched it.

00:00:19.829 --> 00:00:22.260
You have 30 seconds to make your point.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:23.310
- [Narrator] Elon Musk is known

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.410
by his employees to be intense.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.330
When he took over Tesla in 2008,

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.240
he sent the company into crisis
mode to jumpstart his vision

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.253
of how to reinvent the auto industry.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.530
Now he appears to be using

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.740
a similar management
playbook to remake Twitter.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.200
We spoke with former
Tesla and SpaceX employees

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.360
to better understand how Musk became known

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.760
for his cutthroat and
tireless management style

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.343
and what that may mean
for his future employees.

00:00:48.386 --> 00:00:50.130
(audience cheers)

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.260
Step one for Musk is to rally employees

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.846
around a lofty company mission.

00:00:53.846 --> 00:00:55.230
(tool bangs and whirs)

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.330
It's exactly what inspired Carl Medlock

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.400
to join Tesla in 2009.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.020
- We were all so excited about the brand.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.730
I mean, we were all working
80, 100 hours a week.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.850
We kinda lost track of
our families, our passions

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.200
our hobbies, and all that stuff,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.190
but we loved what we were doing.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.770
I never once had Elon or saw Elon do

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.660
a rah-rah meeting like that.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:13.620
- [Narrator] Medlock now runs

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.320
his own electric vehicle
repair shop in Seattle.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.840
He said he was terminated
from Tesla in 2013

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.750
over a misunderstanding
with his direct manager.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.790
Garrett Reisman was similarly inspired

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.790
by the mission of SpaceX
and joined in 2011.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.590
- It's a very demanding environment,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.610
but by and large it's not
because of him cracking the whip.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.170
You know, making human
life multi-planetary,

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.840
getting to Mars, having a colony on Mars.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.670
It's something that motivates

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.500
pretty much everybody there at SpaceX.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.450
- [Narrator] Reisman left SpaceX in 2018

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.160
to teach astronautical engineering

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.290
at the University of Southern California.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.750
He said that after seven years at SpaceX,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.390
he wanted to work somewhere less intense.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.810
Yet at Twitter, the mission
isn't exactly saving the world.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.930
- They are talking about
protecting free speech,

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.490
which of course is important,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.250
but it is not as dramatic
as trying to go to Mars.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.530
- [Narrator] Tim Higgins is a
tech and automotive reporter

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.110
for the "Wall Street Journal"
and author of "Power Play,"

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.260
a book that traces the rise
of Tesla and Elon Musk.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.960
- Well, the challenge
that Elon is going to have

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.170
at Twitter is what are the stakes?

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.730
- [Narrator] Neither Elon Musk nor Twitter

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.950
responded to requests for comment.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.960
The company's mission is what Musk hopes

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.850
will drive people to be hardcore,

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.520
a term he uses to describe
working long hours

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.660
at high intensity.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.970
- You just have to put in, you know,

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.540
80 hour, 80 to 100 hour weeks, every week.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.373
- I would see people
at 2:00 in the morning

00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:35.790
'cause we had finished up an event

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:36.780
and we were all still energized

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.160
'cause we were having such a good time.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.380
- [Narrator] And Musk has
used this idea of hardcore

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.850
to rally his team over the years

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.770
as they approach hard challenges.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.890
For example, in 2012, when
Tesla was preparing to ramp up

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.140
production of the Model S luxury sedan,

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.610
Musk sent his team an email

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.950
with the subject line, "Ultra hardcore,"

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.140
telling them to prepare
for a level of intensity

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.930
that most of them had
never experienced before.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.420
About three weeks after
taking over at Twitter,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.490
Musk sent a similar email,

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.933
but hundreds of employees
opted to leave the company

00:03:04.933 --> 00:03:08.610
rather than sign up for
Musk's vision of Twitter 2.0.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.900
- The challenge here is Elon
has inherited a huge workforce,

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.280
and he has to convince
them to be on Team Elon.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.210
And we're seeing him try to go
through those ranks of people

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.070
to find the talent that he's going to need

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:26.970
to pivot the company to a
private Twitter, and it's messy.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.820
- [Narrator] Musk himself
leads by hardcore example.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:30.810
- I sleep on the couch over there.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.370
Last time I was here I
actually slept, literally,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.170
on the floor 'cause the
couch was too narrow.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.550
- I showed up at work early one day,

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.100
and it was just me and
him and one security guy

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.290
in the entire 5.5 million square feet.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:41.760
And I didn't know he was there, right?

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.813
And at the factory,

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.200
there's walkways and safety
areas and stuff like that.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.033
But when nobody's there,

00:03:47.033 --> 00:03:48.390
I just got on a bicycle, riding around,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:51.120
and here Elon is asleep
on this engineer's desk.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.400
- [Narrator] In November, Musk
tweeted that he was sleeping

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.680
at Twitter's offices in San Francisco.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.110
And it's not just his time, but his money.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.981
- How much did you put into this company?

00:04:00.981 --> 00:04:04.680
- (exhales sharply) Oh,
man, about $55 million.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.210
- In 2008, when Tesla
was at its darkest moment

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.050
and he put in his personal
last millions of dollars

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.170
to fund it, he was even,
you know, couch surfing

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:20.880
in Menlo Park and paying
my business expenses

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.950
with personal credit card debt, right?

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.430
His approach is to do anything required

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.400
to keep his businesses competitive,

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.390
and he lived that every single
day when I worked for him.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.250
- [Narrator] Konrad left Tesla in 2011

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.361
for Renault Nissan Mitsubishi.

00:04:37.361 --> 00:04:39.360
- People that I've talked to with Elon

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.410
talk about how he in one
hand is incredibly inspiring,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.570
but on the other hand a
lot of people will say

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.690
he was also exhausting and mercurial,

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.810
and after a while they just
couldn't keep up with him.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.490
- [Narrator] There is also
a history of employees

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:57.120
at Tesla and SpaceX alleging
misconduct at the companies.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.330
Musk is also known to quickly
make his companies leaner.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.100
When he became CEO of Tesla,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.620
he cut about 20% of the workforce.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.660
About a week after he took over Twitter,

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.090
Musk laid off roughly 50% of the staff

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.340
according to a document
seen by "The Journal."

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.500
And people familiar with
the matter estimated

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.290
that likely over 1000 have resigned.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.550
Former employees have said

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.460
that this cutthroat approach
instills in them this feeling

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.640
that anyone working for Musk
is the best of the best.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:26.490
- Back in the day at
Tesla, Elon would often say

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.580
that people who work for
him are special forces.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.600
- He wants to hire a key
engineer who in his mind

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.720
has way more value than,
say, a hundred engineers,

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.200
and to deploy those top talent
on the hardest problems,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.730
and be able to move faster

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.220
and not have the same kind
of level of bureaucracy.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.480
- [Narrator] Former employees have said

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.330
that Musk avoids traditional
corporate hierarchy.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.550
- If Elon was interested in
something you were doing,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.590
he would come and talk directly to you.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.690
He's not gonna go through
a bunch of different layers

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.270
of management and have
the information filter up.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.670
- [Narrator] Musk also motivates
employees to work harder

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.620
by raising the financial stakes.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.140
- When Elon became CEO of Tesla,

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:05.940
the economy was in shambles.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.360
It was the Great Recession
and there was huge questions

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.720
about whether people were
gonna wanna buy a luxury car,

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.781
and not just a luxury car, a
luxury car that was electric.

00:06:15.781 --> 00:06:16.614
(car door slams)

00:06:16.614 --> 00:06:18.390
Similarities to Twitter today,

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.190
we've got an uncertain economy.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.340
The company needs to start
generating more money.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.170
It has a huge debt load.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:29.490
It has to find its place
in this social media world

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.920
that's under a lot of pressure

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.400
as the ad market, it kind of contracts.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.330
- [Narrator] In November,

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.700
Musk wrote in an email
to Twitter employees

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.950
that without significant
subscription revenue

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.440
there is a good chance
Twitter will not survive

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.510
the upcoming economic downturn.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.420
Musk is still in the early
stages of setting up his strategy

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.700
for Twitter and finding people he trusts

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.160
to help him transform the company.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:55.530
- At SpaceX, if you proved
yourself to be capable,

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:58.260
innovative, especially
on a technical level,

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.960
then that led to very,
very rapid advancement

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:01.980
throughout the company.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:03.630
- If I were an employee
at Twitter right now,

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.493
I'd probably just do your job.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.063
Don't let him know your name.

00:07:08.063 --> 00:07:11.146
(lighthearted music)

